    "answer": "The dataset is not class balanced. The imbalance ratio between the most frequent class (**car**, 2,127 instances) and the least frequent class (**truck**, 381 instances) is **5.58 to 1**.\n\n### Class Distribution\n| Class | Instances | Share (%) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| car | 2127 | 43.79% |\n| person | 1819 | 37.45% |\n| traffic light | 530 | 10.91% |\n| truck | 381 | 7.84% |\n\n### Expected Training Problems\nGiven that the labels were generated by a zero-shot detector without human review, you should expect the following issues when training a detector:\n\n1.  **Class Bias:** The model will likely be biased toward predicting \"car\" and \"person\" due to their significantly higher representation. The \"truck\" and \"traffic light\" classes are under-represented and may suffer from lower recall.\n2.  **Label Noise:** The overall median detector confidence is low (0.43), and approximately 25% of all boxes have a confidence score below 0.35. This indicates high uncertainty in the ground truth labels.\n3.  **Duplicate Detections:** There are **57 pairs** of same-class boxes with an IoU > 0.7, suggesting the detector frequently fired multiple times on the same object.\n4.  **Small Object Noise:** There are **201 boxes** smaller than 32x32 pixels. These are often detector noise rather than genuine instances and may degrade performance if the model learns to predict these artifacts.\n5.  **Domain Inconsistency:** While the dataset is intended for street scenes, it contains close-up photographs where a single object fills the frame. This inconsistency in image composition may confuse the model during training."
